WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.944
Coverage: edge cases and adversarial inputs.

2
00:00:03.224 --> 00:00:08.941
A test set that only contains typical requests will tell you the system handles typical requests.

3
00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:10.779
Plan coverage on purpose.

4
00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:24.819
Anthropic’s eval docs list kinds of edge cases worth including: irrelevant or nonexistent input, overly long input, poor or harmful user input, and ambiguous cases where even people would disagree.

5
00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:27.125
Add to those: Balanced cases.

6
00:00:27.405 --> 00:00:33.101
Anthropic’s evals post warns that one-sided evals produce one-sided optimization.

7
00:00:33.381 --> 00:00:38.672
If every escalation case should hand off, a bot that hands off everything scores perfectly.

8
00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:41.811
Include cases where the behavior must not happen.

9
00:00:42.091 --> 00:00:43.392
Indirect phrasings.

10
00:00:43.672 --> 00:00:49.176
“Please remove everything you have on me” is a privacy request without the word GDPR.

11
00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:56.987
Multi-part requests, where answering one part and dropping the other is a known failure (the lab’s answered_wrong_question mode).

12
00:00:57.267 --> 00:00:58.611
Adversarial inputs.

13
00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:06.208
Users trying to talk the bot past a rule, and prompt-injection attempts in pasted content, which Studio 1 introduced.

14
00:01:06.488 --> 00:01:10.285
Label these with the behavior you expect, just like any other case.

15
00:01:10.565 --> 00:01:18.800
A useful artifact is a coverage matrix: failure modes down the side, dimensions across the top, and a count of cases in each cell.

16
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.579
Empty cells for important modes are where the next cases go.

17
00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:41.461
Chip Huyen’s AI Engineering (chapter 8, on dataset engineering) treats coverage, quantity, and deduplication as separate properties of a dataset, which is a helpful way to review a test set: does it cover the space, is there enough of it, and is it free of near-duplicates that inflate apparent size?
